{
 "id": "2016-middel-outdoor",
 "name": "Outdoor Thermal Comfort in Sun and Shade",
 "description": "On-site thermal comfort questionnaire from Middel et al. (2016, International Journal of Biometeorology), who surveyed 1,284 people in full sun, under photovoltaic canopies and under trees on a pedestrian mall in Tempe, Arizona, across four seasons. Each person answers once (3–5 minutes): health-related mood (5-point), climate familiarity (4 categories), reason for being there, thermal sensation vote on the 9-point scale from very cold (−4) to very hot (+4), comfort (comfortable 0 to very uncomfortable 3), thermal preference (much cooler −3 to much warmer +3), estimated air temperature in sun and shade, where they were and what they did 5 and 30 minutes before (thermal history, e.g. air-conditioning), clothing, gender, age group and sun exposure (full sun / built shade / tree shade). The page stamps the completion time so answers can be linked to your own weather measurements. Analysis as in the paper: compare thermal sensation and comfort between sun, built shade and tree shade (and by season); regress thermal sensation on a measured or modelled thermal index (the paper used PET) to estimate neutral and preferred temperature. Differences from the original study: (1) intermediate labels of the comfort and preference scales follow ISO 10551 (the paper gives the end points and cites ISO 10551); (2) \"shaded by the solar canopy\" is generalised to \"shaded by a built structure\"; (3) climate familiarity wording is adapted from Arizona to \"this climate\"; (4) age groups, clothing items and activity options are our own short lists (the paper converted clothing to clo with ISO 9920 and activity to metabolic rate with ISO 8996, which you do afterwards); (5) no meteorological measurements are included — the researcher records them separately and gives each spot a location code.",
 "author": "Middel, Selover, Hagen & Chhetri",
